# Project 365 — PRD & Architecture Notes
Date: 2026-04-23

---

## What this is

A daily practice repo for frontend interactions, micro-animations, and creative coding
experiments across different languages and frameworks. One experiment per day, each
self-contained, tracked in catalog.json.

---

## Current state

- Git repo: github.com/Abdussalam-popsy/project-365
- Folder structure: YYYY/MM/DD-name/
- catalog.json: populated with all 5 existing experiments
- scripts/new.sh: scaffolds a new experiment from a template
- scripts/catalog-add.sh: adds an entry to catalog.json (supports --date for past experiments, language field)
- Templates: vanilla, canvas, react-vite, next

The scripts and catalog schema are solid. The missing pieces are:
1. Hosting strategy
2. Gallery/index site
3. Personal site integration

---

## Vision

A subpage on the personal site (e.g. yoursite.com/experiments or /365) that acts as a
gallery — browseable by date, filterable by language/tag. Each experiment is a live,
clickable link. The personal site doesn't host the experiments — it just reads
catalog.json and links to wherever each experiment is deployed.

---

## Hosting decision

### Why NOT individual Cloudflare Pages per experiment
- Cloudflare Pages has better use cases (edge functions, Workers, production apps)
- 365+ separate Pages projects becomes unmanageable
- Deployment automation overhead per project is high

### Recommended: GitHub Pages via GitHub Actions (static experiments)
All static experiments (vanilla HTML, Vite/React, canvas) get built and deployed
automatically to GitHub Pages when pushed to main.

Structure:
  github.io/project-365/                          ← gallery index
  github.io/project-365/2026/04/23-cursor-interaction/   ← live experiment

How it works:
- GitHub Action triggers on push to main
- Detects which experiments have changed
- Builds changed experiments (npm run build)
- Outputs to a single dist/ folder mirroring the date structure
- Deploys dist/ to GitHub Pages

This means zero manual deployment — push code, it goes live automatically.

### For non-static experiments (Python, Swift, server-side, etc.)
These need a different host per experiment type:
  Python      → Railway, Render, or Replit (free tier)
  Swift/iOS   → screenshots/video only, or TestFlight
  WASM        → still static, GitHub Pages works

The catalog.json url field handles this — each entry stores whatever URL is live,
regardless of where it's hosted. The gallery just reads the URL.

---

## catalog.json — recommended schema

{
  "date": "2026-04-23",
  "name": "cursor-interaction",
  "path": "2026/04/23-cursor-intercation",
  "url": "https://abdussalam.github.io/project-365/2026/04/23-cursor-intercation",
  "tags": ["canvas", "typescript", "tweakpane"],
  "template": "react-vite",
  "language": "typescript",
  "description": "Isometric shapes that react to cursor proximity",
  "status": "live"
}

Key addition: "language" field — as you learn Python, Go, Swift, etc., this lets the
gallery filter by language. Separate from "template" which is the scaffolding used.

---

## Gallery/index site

Options:
  A. Add a /experiments page to your existing personal site — reads catalog.json
     from the GitHub API and renders a grid.
  B. Build a standalone gallery as another template in this repo — deployed to
     GitHub Pages at the root, automatically updated on push.

Option B is self-contained — no dependency on personal site infrastructure. When
ready, the personal site /experiments page just iframes or mirrors the gallery.

---

## Personal site integration (future)

When the gallery is mature enough to surface on the personal site:
- Personal site fetches catalog.json from GitHub raw URL
- Renders a grid of experiments with date, title, language, tags
- Each card links to the live GitHub Pages URL
- No tight coupling — the catalog is the API

---

## What needs to be built

1. GitHub Action — build + deploy static experiments to GitHub Pages on push
2. scripts/deploy.sh — local script to manually trigger deploy + update catalog url
3. Gallery index site — simple page that reads catalog.json, renders by date
4. ✓ Populate catalog.json — all 5 existing experiments added
5. ✓ Add "language" field + date override to catalog-add.sh script
6. Decide on gallery template (standalone vs. personal site page)

---

## What NOT to do

- Don't host individual experiments on Cloudflare Pages (save for production apps)
- Don't build a heavyweight CMS — catalog.json is the CMS
- Don't over-engineer the gallery — a static HTML/JS page reading JSON is enough
- Don't manually deploy each experiment — automate from the start

---

## Asset loading rules (Vite + GitHub Pages)

GitHub Pages serves experiments from a subdirectory, not the root. This breaks
absolute asset paths. Always follow these rules:

### src/assets/ — import in JS/TS
Vite resolves the path at build time. Works everywhere automatically.

  import logo from './assets/logo.png'
  import './assets/font.css'

Use for: images, icons, fonts referenced in CSS.

### public/ — fetched/loaded at runtime
Must be prefixed with import.meta.env.BASE_URL, which Vite sets to the correct
subdirectory path at build time.

  // ✅ correct
  opentype.load(`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}fonts/MyFont.ttf`)
  fetch(`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}data/config.json`)

  // ❌ breaks on GitHub Pages
  opentype.load('/fonts/MyFont.ttf')
  fetch('/data/config.json')

Use for: fonts loaded dynamically (opentype.js), JSON data files, PDFs, any file
fetched at runtime rather than imported.

Rule of thumb: if you're importing it → src/assets/. If you're fetching it → public/ + BASE_URL.

---

## Notes on learning different languages

As you expand beyond JS/TS:
- Python experiments → package as a web app (Flask/FastAPI) → Railway free tier
- Go → same, or compile to WASM for static hosting
- Swift → record demos, link to GitHub source, no live URL needed
- Anything WASM-compiled → GitHub Pages handles it

The catalog.json "language" + "url" fields accommodate all of this without changing
the gallery structure.
